---
import DistaDocs from "../../../../components/DistaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const description =
  "Dista's tasks: declaring them with up to three arguments, where their results live, freeing results with take and release, tasks on other tasks' results, cancelling and waiting with a timeout, and what can cross the wire.";
---

<DistaDocs href="/s/dista/docs/tasks" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Tasks and results</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A task is a Mojo function that runs on a worker. Calling it returns a reference to a
    result that may not exist yet, which you read, pass to other tasks, or give up on.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#tasks">Tasks</a>
    <a href="#results">Results</a>
    <a href="#freeing">Freeing results</a>
    <a href="#graphs">Tasks on results</a>
    <a href="#giving-up">Cancelling and timeouts</a>
    <a href="#types">What crosses the wire</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="tasks">Tasks</h2>
  <p>
    A task is a top-level <code>def</code>, declared once with its argument and result
    types and registered in the runtime's parameter list. <code>Task</code> takes one
    argument, <code>Task2</code> two and <code>Task3</code> three; more travel as a struct.
    A function that raises declares the same way:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`from dista import Runtime, Task, Task2

def square(x: Int) -> Int:
    return x * x

def describe(x: Int) raises -> String:
    if x < 0:
        raise Error("negative")
    return String("x = ", x)

def add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int:
    return a + b

comptime Square = Task[Int, Int, square]
comptime Describe = Task[Int, String, describe]
comptime Add = Task2[Int, Int, Int, add]

def main() raises:
    var rt = Runtime[Square, Describe, Add](workers=4)
    var r = rt.remote[Square](7)
    print(rt.take(r))                    # 49
    print(rt.take(rt.remote[Add](2, 3))) # 5`} />
  <p>
    <code>rt.remote</code> returns at once with an <code>ObjectRef</code>.
    <code>rt.take</code> and <code>rt.get</code> wait for the result, or raise the task's
    error; <code>rt.ready(r)</code> asks without waiting. Every process in a cluster runs
    the same binary, so a task is named on the wire by a hash of the function, and a
    process built with different tasks is refused when it connects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <p>
    A small result, 64 KiB or less, comes back to the driver through the head. A larger one
    stays on the worker that computed it: in a shared-memory file on that host, which a
    reader on the same host maps without copying, or in the worker's memory, which a
    reader elsewhere fetches from that worker directly. <code>rt.location(r)</code> says
    where a result lives: the worker's <code>ip:port</code>, then its file if it has one,
    or nothing if it came back with the reply.
  </p>
  <p>
    A result larger than 64 MiB crosses hosts in parts, so results past 1 GiB fetch too.
    A task's argument has to fit one frame: past 1 GiB, <code>remote</code> raises. Large
    data belongs in results, which stay on the workers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="freeing">Freeing results</h2>
  <p>
    A result is kept in the driver, at the head and on its worker until the driver releases
    its ref. <code>rt.take(r)</code> is <code>get</code> then <code>release</code>, the way
    to read a result used once, which is most of them; <code>rt.take(refs)</code> takes a
    list in order. <code>rt.get(r)</code> keeps the result, for reading again, until
    <code>rt.release(r)</code>. A loop that only calls <code>get</code> grows all three
    by every result.
  </p>
  <p>
    The head counts references to every result: the driver's own, until it's released,
    and one for every pending task that takes it as input. At zero the result is deleted
    wherever it is, so releasing a ref that a submitted task still needs is fine.
    <code>rt.stats()</code> reports what the head holds, and <code>dista status</code>
    shows how many objects each driver holds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="graphs">Tasks on results</h2>
  <p>
    A task can take another task's result instead of a value. The head runs it once the
    input exists, and the worker running it fetches the input from wherever it lives. A
    list of refs is gathered, in order, into a <code>Seq</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`from dista import ObjectRef, Runtime, Seq, Task

def total(xs: Seq[Int]) -> Int:
    var t = 0
    for i in range(len(xs)):
        t += xs[i]
    return t

comptime Total = Task[Seq[Int], Int, total]

var maps = List[ObjectRef[Int]]()
for i in range(1000):
    maps.append(rt.remote[Square](i))
var sum = rt.remote[Total](maps)   # runs once all 1,000 exist
rt.release(maps)                   # only Total needs them, and it still gets them
var next = rt.remote[Square](sum)  # a task on a task's result
print(rt.take(next))`} />
  <p>
    If an input fails, every task that depends on it fails with that error, and never
    runs. A task given a ref takes it whole: a ref nested inside a struct is a compile
    error. The ways to pass refs are one ref, a list gathered into a <code>Seq</code>,
    and a value beside a list, which <a href="/s/dista/docs/shuffles">shuffles</a> use.
  </p>

  <h2 id="giving-up">Cancelling and timeouts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rt.cancel(r)</code> gives up on a result. A task that hasn't run never does; one
    that's running finishes on its worker, which can't be interrupted, and its result is
    dropped. <code>get</code> then raises <code>cancelled</code>, and so does every task
    waiting on <code>r</code>. A result already in the driver is kept. An actor call is
    answered <code>cancelled</code> but still runs, since the calls after it expect its
    effect. <code>rt.cancel(refs)</code> cancels a list; a cancelled ref is released like
    any other.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`var r = rt.remote[Slow](x)
try:
    print(rt.get(r, timeout=30.0))
except e:
    # "timed out after 30.0 s waiting for ...": r is still valid
    rt.cancel(r)
    rt.release(r)`} />
  <p>
    <code>rt.get(r, timeout=s)</code> raises an error starting with <code>timed out</code>
    once <code>s</code> seconds pass, leaving the ref valid, to wait on again, cancel or
    release. <code>get(r)</code> without a timeout waits as long as it takes. A time limit
    on the task itself, however its worker stalls, is
    <a href="/s/dista/docs/failures#timeouts"><code>DISTA_TASK_TIMEOUT</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="types">What crosses the wire</h2>
  <p>
    Arguments and results are encoded by type, at compile time. Scalars, <code>Bool</code>
    and <code>String</code> have fixed encodings, and any other struct is encoded field by
    field. Arrow <code>RecordBatch</code>es from arrow-mlake travel as Arrow IPC, so
    columnar data keeps its format between tasks. A type that needs its own encoding
    implements the <code>Codec</code> trait, an <code>encode(self, mut out)</code> and a
    static <code>decode(buf, mut pos)</code>. A bare <code>List</code> doesn't encode: send
    a <code>Seq</code>, a struct, or Arrow instead. Both ends are the same binary, so there's
    no schema evolution to manage.
  </p>
</DistaDocs>
